%[text] # Presenting Tropical Cyclone Paths
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] ## Download the Data
%[text] [The LoadStormData script](<matlab: edit LoadStormData.m>) in the FunctionLibrary folder downloads, processes, and saves storm track data from the [NOAA Hurricane database](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/hurdat/) into the Data folder. 
LoadStormData
%%
%[text] 1\) Choose the storm you want to track by selecting a value from 1 to 1005.
storm = 1; %[control:editfield:83d7]{"position":[9,10]}
load allStorms.mat
myStorm = allStorms(storm);
%[text] ![](text:image:611c)Choose various storms such as:
%[text] 1\) Annette, storm number 8
%[text] 2\) Miriam, storm number 301
%[text] 3\) Olivia, storm number 818
%[text] 4\) Raymond, storm number 840
%[text] 5\) Norbert, storm number 1003
disp("My storm is named "+myStorm.Name) %[output:7154c9d4]
%%
%[text] ## Plot the Storm Track
% Plot the path of the storm on a map using the latitude and longitude
% values. Can you tell how the storm was moving?
mapOfStorm = geoplot(myStorm.Lat,myStorm.Long,"*"); %[output:2135a00a]
title(myStorm.Name + ", " + myStorm.Year) %[output:2135a00a]
startingMonth = month(myStorm.StartTime,"name");
endingMonth = month(myStorm.EndTime,"name");
subtitle(startingMonth{1} + " " + day(myStorm.StartTime) + " to " ... %[output:group:1b527f46] %[output:2135a00a]
    + endingMonth{1} + " " + day(myStorm.EndTime)) %[output:group:1b527f46] %[output:2135a00a]
%%
%[text] ## Animate the Storm Track
% Choose an appropriate zoom level to see the entire storm track and
% animate the path of the storm. Which direction do you think it is moving?
zoomLevel = 5; %[control:slider:8599]{"position":[13,14]}
player = geoplayer(mean(myStorm.Lat),mean(myStorm.Long),zoomLevel,"HistoryDepth",Inf); %[output:765ae11b]
for i = 1:length(myStorm.Lat) %[output:group:53c6f019]
    plotPosition(player,myStorm.Lat(i),myStorm.Long(i)); %[output:765ae11b]
    pause(0.1)
end %[output:group:53c6f019]
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:2bac) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. What type of curve do you want to use to describe the path of a tropical cyclone?
%[text] 2. What type of curve do you want to use to predict the path of a tropical cyclone?
%[text] 3. How might you try to test your predictions against reality? \
%%
%[text] ## Add detail to the storm track
%[text] You can use the built-in `spline` or `pchip` interpolators or use your own. 
% Set up a time-index value in units of hours with the understanding that
% the original data points exist every 6 hours
indexVals = ((1:length(myStorm.Lat))-1)*6;

% Determine the interpolation values
timeInHrs = 0:.2:hours(myStorm.EndTime-myStorm.StartTime);
latInterp = spline(indexVals,myStorm.Lat,timeInHrs); %[control:dropdown:6fa2]{"position":[13,19]}
longInterp = spline(indexVals,myStorm.Long,timeInHrs); %[control:dropdown:62c5]{"position":[14,20]}

% Plot the more detailed interpolated path
mapOfStorm;
hold on %[output:4a98775e]
geoplot(latInterp,longInterp,"-") %[output:4a98775e]
hold off %[output:4a98775e]
%[text] Choose an appropriate zoom level to see the storm track and animate the path of the storm. 
%[text] ![](text:image:8ce0) **Reflect**
%[text] 1. Which direction do you think it is moving?
%[text] 2. If you tried to extrapolate the path of the storm, were you correct?
%[text] 3. When you interpolate between known locations for the storm, do you believe the path you create? \
zoomLevel = 5; %[control:slider:10fa]{"position":[13,14]}
player = geoplayer(mean(myStorm.Lat),mean(myStorm.Long),zoomLevel,"HistoryDepth",Inf); %[output:65be8ff9]
for i = 1:length(latInterp) %[output:group:908999ee]
    plotPosition(player,latInterp(i),longInterp(i)); %[output:65be8ff9]
    pause(0.01)
end %[output:group:908999ee]
